        {
            "timestamp": 1594700641,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                "username": "jk-ozlabs"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 4:\n\nI'm not clear on the failure mode you're looking to address here. You said:\n\n> attempts to transmit packets is likely to fail again\n\nbut also:\n\n> This blocks any further transmission of packets\n\nbut if packets are likely to fail again, what exactly is blocked?\n\nI do agree that we want to handle one-off failures gracefully, and allow recovery. However, as you mention:\n\n> 2) In case of tx_fail, notify the caller that tx failed so that the caller can retry.\n\nisn't this just pushing the problem over to the caller? How does it have any better knowledge of the binding states?"
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1594702482,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Sumanth Bhat",
                "email": "sumanth.bhat@linux.intel.com",
                "username": "sumbhat90"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 4:\n\n(1 comment)\n\n> but if packets are likely to fail again, what exactly is blocked?\n\nThe failed packet is kept in the queue forever, and txqueue fails to transmit the packet and breaks. This blocks transmission of any packet which is further down the queue."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1594703062,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Sumanth Bhat",
                "email": "sumanth.bhat@linux.intel.com",
                "username": "sumbhat90"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 4:\n\n> isn't this just pushing the problem over to the caller? How does it\n > have any better knowledge of the binding states?\n\nAgree, caller doesn't know the knowledge of the binding states; however caller would like to know if tx was successful or not. That would enable the caller to retry or not (left to the implementer). \n\nTake an edge case scenario - Add-in card with a SMBus address may be plugged out just before packet transmission. So we end up with a packet with an invalid MCTP over SMBus packet in the queue. So the behaviour I've introduced in the patch is to drop this invalid packet and let the caller know that packet transmission failed (So the caller is aware that target EID is no longer valid)."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1594952075,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Richard Marian Thomaiyar",
                "email": "richard.marian.thomaiyar@linux.intel.com",
                "username": "rthomaiy"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 4:\n\n(4 comments)\n\n> Patch Set 4:\n> \n> I'm not clear on the failure mode you're looking to address here. You said:\n> \n> > attempts to transmit packets is likely to fail again\n> \n> but also:\n> \n> > This blocks any further transmission of packets\n> \n> but if packets are likely to fail again, what exactly is blocked?\n> \n> I do agree that we want to handle one-off failures gracefully, and allow recovery. However, as you mention:\n> \n> > 2) In case of tx_fail, notify the caller that tx failed so that the caller can retry.\n> \n> isn't this just pushing the problem over to the caller? How does it have any better knowledge of the binding states?\n\n[Richard]: Not really. Caller must anyway needs to monitor the packet & handle it based on timeout. In other words, if we don't say immediate failure if we have any, then application/ caller must wait till timeout. At the same time, the current place we return the error is not guarnteed and will end up causing more confusion, as we will not know, when things will be added in queue."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1594975950,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Sumanth Bhat",
                "email": "sumanth.bhat@linux.intel.com",
                "username": "sumbhat90"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 4:\n\n(1 comment)\n\n> (4 comments)\n > \n > > Patch Set 4:\n > >\n > > I'm not clear on the failure mode you're looking to address here.\n > You said:\n > >\n > > > attempts to transmit packets is likely to fail again\n > >\n > > but also:\n > >\n > > > This blocks any further transmission of packets\n > >\n > > but if packets are likely to fail again, what exactly is blocked?\n > >\n > > I do agree that we want to handle one-off failures gracefully,\n > and allow recovery. However, as you mention:\n > >\n > > > 2) In case of tx_fail, notify the caller that tx failed so that\n > the caller can retry.\n > >\n > > isn't this just pushing the problem over to the caller? How does\n > it have any better knowledge of the binding states?\n > \n > [Richard]: Not really. Caller must anyway needs to monitor the\n > packet & handle it based on timeout. In other words, if we don't\n > say immediate failure if we have any, then application/ caller must\n > wait till timeout. At the same time, the current place we return\n > the error is not guarnteed and will end up causing more confusion,\n > as we will not know, when things will be added in queue.\n\nWould the following change be more agreeable ?\nI will drop a single packet for which transmission failed and report the same to the caller.\nThis will mean that destination endpoint might miss one packet in the transmitted message, but the miss was only due to tx error and thus this behavior is okay.\n\nFor packets whose transmission were held up due to tx being disabled will return queued packet's \"unique ID\" to the caller. The caller can probe the packet's tx status at any time using the packet's unique id."
        },
        {
            "timestamp": 1594976386,
            "reviewer": {
                "name": "Sumanth Bhat",
                "email": "sumanth.bhat@linux.intel.com",
                "username": "sumbhat90"
            },
            "message": "Patch Set 4:\n\n(1 comment)"
        },
